## 3. Detailed Detector/Systematics Attack

### 3.1 BEST Detector Geometry & Source Positioning

Mechanism:  
The BEST experiment consists of a cylindrical tank of liquid gallium with a central \(^{51}\text{Cr}\) source. The active volume is divided into an inner spherical zone and an outer shell. Any deviation from perfect symmetry – dead layers, the source guide tube, support structures, temperature gradients – breaks the ideal isotropy and introduces a directional response.

Faking LSC:  
If the neutrino detection efficiency varies with direction due to geometric asymmetries (e.g., more passive material in one hemisphere), the effective detector response is no longer a scalar. The LSC tensor will naturally fit a non‑zero traceless component whose axis aligns with the tank’s symmetry axis (e.g., vertical). This appears as a “directional anisotropy” in the detector frame but is purely geometrical.

Severity: MEDIUM  
BEST is carefully designed to be symmetric, and many asymmetries average out in the counting phase. However, sub‑percent residual effects can match the size of the anomaly (~20% deficit), especially if correlated with the inner/outer zone division.

Separating test:  
- Map the detection efficiency as a function of spatial position and direction using a calibrated radioactive source moved through the tank before it is filled (or using a dummy source).  
- Rotate the entire tank (or source) by 180° about a horizontal axis and see if the fitted anisotropy vector flips sign or stays fixed. If it stays fixed in the laboratory, it is a hardware artefact.

