### 3.2 Inner vs Outer Zone Effects & Extraction Bias

Mechanism:  
After exposure, the \(^{71}\text{Ge}\) atoms are chemically extracted. The extraction efficiency can differ between the inner and outer zones because of different mixing, recovery times, or plumbing dead volumes. Even small (few‑percent) differentials can mimic a radial dependence of the signal.

Faking LSC:  
LSC’s traceless anisotropy, when expressed in detector coordinates, will naturally pick up any radial trend (inner rate ≠ outer rate). The tensor can represent this as a “directional” effect because the inner zone is geometrically distinct from the outer zone. A genuine neutrino oscillation would also produce an inner/outer difference, but an extraction bias would mimic it perfectly.

Severity: CRITICAL  
The BEST collaboration already applies corrections, but residual uncertainties of a few percent are plausible. A systematic extraction difference of ~3–5% between zones could entirely explain the observed inner/outer ratio anomaly and, consequently, the fitted traceless term.
