### 3.3 Calibration: Source Activity & Cross‑Section

Mechanism:  
The expected capture rate is proportional to the product of source activity, number of target nuclei, and the cross‑section. The \(^{51}\text{Cr}\) activity is typically measured via gamma‑ray counting, and the \(^{71}\text{Ga}(\nu_e,e^-)^{71}\text{Ge}\) cross‑section relies on nuclear matrix elements with ~2–3% theoretical uncertainty. A systematic underestimation of the cross‑section or overestimation of the source activity would produce a deficit.

Faking LSC:  
A smooth, energy‑dependent miscalibration of the cross‑section is indistinguishable from the LSC trace term \(E_\text{rec} = E_\text{true}(1+\delta)\). Since the Ga anomaly is observed at essentially two discrete energy lines (from \(^{51}\text{Cr}\) and \(^{37}\text{Ar}\)), a single multiplicative correction per isotope is sufficient to explain the deficit. The LSC trace correction would simply absorb that.

Severity: CRITICAL  
This is the most parsimonious explanation: a ~3% mistake in the cross‑section normalisation would remove the whole anomaly.

Separating test:  
- Measure the cross‑section independently with a different target nucleus (e.g., a high‑precision liquid‑scintillator measurement of inverse‑beta decay at a reactor) that has a well‑known cross‑section, thereby anchoring the gallium cross‑section.  
- Directly determine the source activity using calorimetry, 4π ionisation chambers, and multiple gamma‑ray spectrometers to <0.5% precision.

