### 3.4 Environmental Drift (Temperature, Radon, …)

Mechanism:  
The \(^{71}\text{Ge}\) counting rate is extremely low (order of a few atoms per day). The background consists of cosmic‑ray induced events, radon, electronic noise, etc., and depends on environmental conditions (temperature, pressure, humidity, radon concentration). If the background varies systematically over the run, the subtracted signal inherits a time‑modulation.

Faking LSC:  
Without timestamps, the LSC fit collapses all data into a single rate per energy/exposure. A time‑varying background that correlates with the source‑on periods or with sidereal/diurnal cycles can produce a static residual that, when parameterised with a tensor, yields a non‑zero anisotropy. If the environmental drift correlates with the laboratory orientation (e.g., warmer during the day), the fitted vector will align with the lab’s daily rotation axis, masquerading as a directional effect.

Severity: CRITICAL  
Background systematics are the dominant uncertainty in low‑rate radiochemical experiments. A plausible, untracked drift of 0.1 counts per day can fake the entire anomaly.

Separating test:  
- Publish full timestamped event lists and environmental sensor logs (temperature, radon, atmospheric pressure) for every exposure.  
- Split the data into day/night and winter/summer subsets and fit the LSC tensor separately; if the anisotropy changes, it is environmental.  
- Perform a background‑only run (with no source) for a statistically equivalent period to measure the time‑structure empirically.

